 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "Prepositions that take the ablative",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "The prepositions in Chapter 1 took the accusative, and most of them meant movement towards something. These take the <b>ablative</b>. They mostly mean being somewhere, or moving away from it."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servus <u class=\"c\">cum</u> <u class=\"v\">amico</u> ambulat.",
      "en": "The slave walks <b>with a friend</b>.",
      "note": "<em>cum</em> takes the ablative: <em>amico</em>, not <em>amicum</em>."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "The noun after <em>a, ab, cum, de, e, ex</em> and <em>in</em> meaning \"in\" is in the ablative: <em>-a</em> or <em>-o</em> in the singular, <em>-is</em> in the plural.",
      "<em>a</em> and <em>e</em> are used before a consonant, <em>ab</em> and <em>ex</em> before a vowel: <em>a villa</em>, <em>ab insula</em>; <em>e villa</em>, <em>ex insula</em>.",
      "Translate the preposition and its noun together, as one phrase."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "list",
   "h2": "The prepositions",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       "Latin",
       "English",
       "Example"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "<em>a, ab</em>",
        "from, away from",
        "<em>a villa</em>: away from the house"
       ],
       [
        "<em>cum</em>",
        "with, together with",
        "<em>cum servis</em>: with the slaves"
       ],
       [
        "<em>de</em>",
        "down from, about",
        "<em>de muro</em>: down from the wall"
       ],
       [
        "<em>e, ex</em>",
        "out of, from",
        "<em>e taberna</em>: out of the shop"
       ],
       [
        "<em>in</em>",
        "in, on",
        "<em>in horto</em>: in the garden"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "note": "There are two ways to say \"with\". <em>cum</em> means \"together with\", usually a person: <em>cum amico</em>. The ablative on its own means \"with\" a thing you use: <em>gladio</em>, \"with a sword\"."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "in",
   "h2": "The two meanings of <em>in</em>",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "<em>in</em> takes either case, and the case changes the meaning. With the accusative it is movement, \"into\". With the ablative it is position, \"in\" or \"on\"."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servi <u class=\"c\">in</u> <u class=\"v\">horto</u> laborant.",
      "en": "The slaves are working <b>in the garden</b>.",
      "note": "<em>horto</em> is ablative, so they are in the garden already."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servi in hortum ambulant.",
      "en": "The slaves walk <b>into the garden</b>.",
      "note": "<em>hortum</em> is accusative, so they are going into it."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Translating the phrase",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "Find the preposition, then the noun after it. Check the noun's case only when the preposition is <em>in</em>.",
      "Keep the two together in English: \"out of the shop\", \"with his friends\".",
      "Latin often leaves out \"his\" or \"her\". Add it when English sounds better with it."
     ]
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "nuntius <u class=\"c\">e</u> <u class=\"v\">villa</u> ambulat.",
      "en": "The messenger walks <b>out of the house</b>."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servus <u class=\"c\">de</u> <u class=\"v\">muro</u> clamat.",
      "en": "The slave shouts <b>down from the wall</b>."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "dominus <u class=\"c\">cum</u> <u class=\"v\">amicis</u> ambulat.",
      "en": "The master walks <b>with his friends</b>.",
      "note": "<em>amicis</em> is ablative plural. \"His\" is added for the English."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "<em>in</em> with the ablative is \"in\" or \"on\". With the accusative it is \"into\". Look at the ending of the noun.",
      "<em>e, ex</em> is \"out of\"; <em>a, ab</em> is \"away from\". Both can be \"from\".",
      "<em>cum</em> is \"with\" a person. A thing used, like <em>gladio</em>, needs no preposition.",
      "<em>de</em> is \"down from\" with a place, and \"about\" with a story or a letter."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
